One hour after the fleet rollout (15:43 to 16:38 UTC, nine duals at 155 counted with three batches, 30 s discovery horizon):
429 share per node 0 to 0.1% (sg2 12.4% to 0.1%, its Shopify 429s 41.5% to 0.4%; par1 4.6% to 0.09%; ny2 2.8% to 0.05%). Fleet 2xx rows 228/min vs 214/min on the old code 13:00-14:00. Rows/min per node 32.9-38.7 but that includes refusal rows. BEAM RSS 434-996 MB per dual, h1 1.5 GB. Queue 25K, inflow 506/min lifetime, drain 284/min.
Found: the recrawl scheduler's tier A tick (4,467 at priority 0 every 30 min, :04 and :34) is Shopify-heavy; every node then starts batches with far more Shopify stores than the 2 s edge line can book inside 30 s (16 per batch start). First refusal: no row, back to the tail of priority 0, picked up inside the same wave, refused again, recorded as rate_limited and shelved for the cadence. 835 / 715 such rows in the 15:45 and 16:15 quarter hours, all 23.227.38.x, ~45% of the tick's Shopify stores; none fetched again since. The old code lost ~300-540 per wave as 429 + rate_limited rows, so 429s are gone but shelved stores doubled. Between waves the edge line sits idle (backlog negative on every node). Fix proposed, not applied: run edge-shared targets in a small sub-stream (concurrency ~4 per batch) so bookings stay inside the horizon and the line is fed for the whole stage, and/or drip the tier A tick over its 30 minutes.

Six hours after the rollout (15:43 to 21:38 UTC): 429 share per node 0.02-0.19% (sg2 0.19%, its Shopify 429s 0.38%); fleet 429 rows 10-22 per hour vs 192-591 on the old code. Fleet 2xx rows 225-232/min for 16:00-21:00 vs 195-221 on the old code 09:00-14:00. New sites first fetched per hour 4,700-5,600 vs 3,400-3,700 before (part of that is the queue draining from 32K to 9.8K; it now sits at priority depth 0, input-bound). Email yield per 2xx row 36-39%, unchanged. BEAM RSS 640-921 MB per dual on three batches, h1 1.29 GB. The tier A wave problem persists every 30 min: 350-710 stores recorded as rate_limited per wave (890-1,261 per hour), and 3 of the thousands refused twice before 18:30 were fetched again afterwards. Fix still proposed, not applied.
